use std
into frame.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with fr_ is exported). It analyzes three-dimensional beam frames (joints joined by straight members of standard cross sections) with the direct stiffness method: section properties, static deflections, support reactions, member forces and stresses along every member, and the linear buckling load factor with its mode shape; and it sizes bolted joints after VDI 2230. Units are millimeters, newtons, newton-millimeters and megapascals. Its only import line is `import math;`, ending with a semicolon. Every int used in real math is converted with `as real`, and a real used as an int with `as int`, since Helium has no real or int conversion calls.

```amni
extern fn fr_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "fr_view_f64";
extern fn fr_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "fr_view_i32";
```

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `NJ` (joints), `NMB` (members), `NSC` (sections), `NS` (elements per member), `NT` (nodes), `NE` (elements), `NQ` (equations) and `NV` (skyline entries). Growable f64 arrays: `IN` (64 inputs), `OUT` (64 outputs), `XJ` (3 per joint: x, y, z), `MW` (4 per member: roll angle in degrees, then the load per length along global x, y and z), `SC` (8 per section: shape code, then b, h, t, tf, tw), `SP` (8 per section: A, Iy, Iz, J, cy, cz, Zt, round flag), `FJ` (6 per joint: Fx, Fy, Fz, Mx, My, Mz), `UJ` (6 per joint displacement), `RJ` (6 per joint reaction), `ST` (8 per member station: N, Vy, Vz, T, My, Mz, sigma, von Mises), `SD` (3 per member station, static displacement), `SB` (3 per member station, buckling mode), `MS` (8 per member summary), `XN` (3 per node), `LAM` (9 per member, rotation rows), `PE` (1 per element, axial force), `KV`, `KS` and `GV` (`NV` skyline entries each), `FV`, `UV`, `XV`, `YV`, `ZV` and `WV` (1 per equation each), `KE`, `GE` and `TE` (144 each), `FE`, `FG`, `DE`, `QE` and `CV` (12 each). Growable i32 arrays: `MB` (4 per member: joint i, joint j, section, release bits), `SU` (6 per joint, 1 where held), `EN` (3 per element: node a, node b, member), `RL` (1 per element, release bits), `POS` (node position in the equation order), `DEG`, `SEEN`, `ORD`, `CUR` (1 per node each), `AS` (node count plus 1), `AJ` (2 per element), `TOP` (1 per equation), `CP` (equations plus 1), `RC` and `EC` (1 per joint each).

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n, x)` does the same for an i32 growable array, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. On the web an array that holds entries cannot grow past the room it first reserved, so every array is emptied before it is sized again.

The exported function `fr_views()`, declared with the export keyword, registers the arrays JavaScript reads or writes through fr_view_f64 and fr_view_i32: f64 views 1 `IN`, 2 `OUT`, 3 `XJ`, 4 `MW`, 5 `SC`, 7 `SP`, 8 `FJ`, 9 `UJ`, 10 `RJ`, 11 `ST`, 12 `SD`, 13 `SB`, 14 `MS`; i32 views 20 `MB`, 21 `SU`. It returns 0.

The exported function `fr_init()`, declared with the export keyword, sizes with the helpers `IN` and `OUT` to 64 zeros, every other f64 array to 1 zero and every i32 array to 1 zero, calls `fr_views` and returns 0.

The exported function `fr_size(nj, nm, ns, nseg)`, declared with the export keyword, takes four i32 values. It sets `NJ` to nj, `NMB` to nm, `NSC` to ns and `NS` to the larger of nseg and 1. With k equal to nm times (`NS` plus 1), it sizes with the helpers, raising every size of 0 to 1: `XJ` to 3 nj zeros, `FJ`, `UJ` and `RJ` to 6 nj zeros, `MW` to 4 nm zeros, `SC` and `SP` to 8 ns zeros, `ST` to 8 k zeros, `SD` and `SB` to 3 k zeros, `MS` to 8 nm zeros, `MB` to 4 nm zeros and `SU` to 6 nj zeros. It calls `fr_views` and returns 0.

`sec_props(s)` takes an i32 section and fills the 8 entries of `SP` from 8 s from the shape in `SC` from 8 s: k is SC[8 s] as int, then b, h, t, tf and tw are SC[8 s plus 1] to SC[8 s plus 5]. The results A, Iy, Iz, J, cy, cz, Zt and rnd (round flag, 1.0 or 0.0) go to SP[8 s] to SP[8 s plus 7] in that order; pi is the math constant. Shape 0, solid rectangle b wide and h deep: A is b times h, Iy is b times h cubed over 12, Iz is h times b cubed over 12, with ra the larger of b and h halved and rc the smaller halved, J is ra times rc cubed times (16 over 3 minus 3.36 times rc over ra times (1 minus rc to the fourth over (12 times ra to the fourth))), cy is b over 2, cz is h over 2, Zt is 8 times ra squared times rc squared over (3 ra plus 1.8 rc), rnd 0.0. Shape 1, round bar of diameter b: r is b over 2, A is pi r squared, Iy and Iz are pi r to the fourth over 4, J is pi r to the fourth over 2, cy and cz are r, Zt is J over r, rnd 1.0. Shape 2, round tube of outer diameter b and wall t: ro is b over 2, ri is the larger of ro minus t and 0.0, A is pi times (ro squared minus ri squared), Iy and Iz are pi times (ro to the fourth minus ri to the fourth) over 4, J is twice Iy, cy and cz are ro, Zt is J over ro, rnd 1.0. Shape 3, rectangular tube b by h with wall t: bi is the larger of b minus 2 t and 0.0, hi is the larger of h minus 2 t and 0.0, A is b h minus bi hi, Iy is (b times h cubed minus bi times hi cubed) over 12, Iz is (h times b cubed minus hi times bi cubed) over 12, J is 2 t times (b minus t) squared times (h minus t) squared over (b plus h minus 2 t), cy is b over 2, cz is h over 2, Zt is 2 t times (b minus t) times (h minus t), rnd 0.0. Shape 4, I-beam with flange width b, depth h, flange thickness tf and web thickness tw: hw is h minus 2 tf, A is 2 b tf plus hw tw, Iy is (b times h cubed minus (b minus tw) times hw cubed) over 12, Iz is (2 tf times b cubed plus hw times tw cubed) over 12, J is (2 b times tf cubed plus hw times tw cubed) over 3, cy is b over 2, cz is h over 2, Zt is J over the larger of tf and tw, rnd 0.0. Shape 5, channel with flange width b (including the web), depth h, flange thickness tf and web thickness tw: hw is h minus 2 tf, A is 2 b tf plus hw tw, ey is (b squared times tf plus hw times tw squared over 2) over A, Iy is (b times h cubed minus (b minus tw) times hw cubed) over 12, Iz is 2 times (tf times b cubed over 12 plus b tf times (b over 2 minus ey) squared) plus hw times tw cubed over 12 plus hw tw times (tw over 2 minus ey) squared, J is (2 b times tf cubed plus hw times tw cubed) over 3, cy is the larger of ey and b minus ey, cz is h over 2, Zt is J over the larger of tf and tw, rnd 0.0. Any other shape (7 is a custom section that JavaScript filled in) leaves `SP` as it is. Returns 0.

The exported function `fr_sections()`, declared with the export keyword, calls `sec_props`(s) for every section s below `NSC` and returns 0.

`axes(m)` takes an i32 member and returns its length as a real, storing its rotation rows in `LAM` from 9 m. i is MB[4 m] and j is MB[4 m plus 1]; dx, dy, dz are XJ[3 j plus c] minus XJ[3 i plus c] for c 0, 1, 2, and L is the square root of their squares summed. When L is 0.0 the 9 entries become the identity rows (1,0,0), (0,1,0), (0,0,1) and it returns 0.0. Otherwise ex is (dx, dy, dz) over L. The reference r is (1, 0, 0) when the absolute value of the z part of ex is above 0.999 and (0, 0, 1) otherwise. With rd the dot product of r and ex, ez0 is r minus rd times ex, then divided by its length. ey0 is the cross product of ez0 and ex: (ez0y exz minus ez0z exy, ez0z exx minus ez0x exz, ez0x exy minus ez0y exx). With bt equal to MW[4 m] times pi over 180, ey is cos(bt) times ey0 plus sin(bt) times ez0, and ez is cos(bt) times ez0 minus sin(bt) times ey0. `LAM` from 9 m gets ex in its entries 0 to 2, ey in 3 to 5 and ez in 6 to 8. Returns L.

`node_of(m, s)` takes an i32 member and an i32 station from 0 to `NS` and returns MB[4 m] when s is 0, MB[4 m plus 1] when s is `NS`, and otherwise `NJ` plus m times (`NS` minus 1) plus s minus 1.

`build()` sets `NT` to `NJ` plus `NMB` times (`NS` minus 1) and `NE` to `NMB` times `NS`, and sizes with the helpers, raising every size of 0 to 1: `XN` to 3 `NT` zeros, `LAM` to 9 `NMB` zeros, `EN` to 3 `NE` zeros, `RL` to `NE` zeros and `PE` to `NE` zeros. XN[k] copies XJ[k] for k below 3 `NJ`. For each member m: MS[8 m] becomes `axes`(m); i is MB[4 m], j is MB[4 m plus 1] and rb is MB[4 m plus 3]; for each k from 1 to `NS` minus 1 the node nd equal to `NJ` plus m times (`NS` minus 1) plus k minus 1 gets XN[3 nd plus c] equal to XJ[3 i plus c] plus (XJ[3 j plus c] minus XJ[3 i plus c]) times k over `NS` for c 0, 1, 2. For each s from 0 to `NS` minus 1, the element e equal to m times `NS` plus s gets EN[3 e] equal to node_of(m, s), EN[3 e plus 1] equal to node_of(m, s plus 1) and EN[3 e plus 2] equal to m; RL[e] starts at 0, gains 1 when s is 0 and rb bitwise-and 1 is not 0, gains 2 when s is `NS` minus 1 and rb bitwise-and 2 is not 0, and gains 4 when s is `NS` minus 1 and rb is 3. Returns 0.

`bfs(s, c0)` takes two i32 values, a start node and a slot in `ORD`, and returns the slot after the last node it reached. ORD[c0] becomes s and SEEN[s] becomes 1; hd is c0 and tl is c0 plus 1. While hd is below tl: n is ORD[hd] and hd grows by 1; t0 is tl; for each p from AS[n] up to AS[n plus 1] minus 1, q is AJ[p], and when SEEN[q] is 0, SEEN[q] becomes 1, ORD[tl] becomes q and tl grows by 1. Then the entries of `ORD` from t0 to tl minus 1 are sorted by insertion so their DEG values increase, keeping the order of equal ones. Returns tl.

`order()` numbers the nodes in reverse Cuthill-McKee order. It sizes with the helpers `DEG`, `SEEN`, `ORD`, `POS` and `CUR` to `NT` zeros, `AS` to `NT` plus 1 zeros and `AJ` to 2 `NE` zeros (at least 1). For each element e with a equal to EN[3 e] and b equal to EN[3 e plus 1], when a differs from b, DEG[a] and DEG[b] each grow by 1. AS[n plus 1] is AS[n] plus DEG[n] for each n, and CUR[n] copies AS[n]. For each element with a differing from b: AJ[CUR[a]] becomes b and CUR[a] grows by 1, then AJ[CUR[b]] becomes a and CUR[b] grows by 1. cnt is 0. While cnt is below `NT`: st is the node with SEEN 0 whose DEG is smallest (the lowest such node on ties); e1 is bfs(st, cnt); la is ORD[e1 minus 1]; SEEN[ORD[k]] becomes 0 for each k from cnt to e1 minus 1; cnt becomes bfs(la, cnt). Finally POS[ORD[k]] becomes `NT` minus 1 minus k for each k below `NT`. Returns 0.

`profile()` sets `NQ` to 6 `NT` and sizes with the helpers `TOP` to `NQ` zeros and `CP` to `NQ` plus 1 zeros. TOP[q] starts at q. For each element e with nodes a and b, lo is 6 times the smaller of POS[a] and POS[b]; for each k below 6, TOP[6 POS[a] plus k] and TOP[6 POS[b] plus k] each become the smaller of their value and lo. CP[q plus 1] is CP[q] plus q minus TOP[q] plus 1, and `NV` becomes CP[`NQ`]. It sizes with the helpers `KV`, `KS` and `GV` to `NV` zeros and `FV`, `UV`, `XV`, `YV`, `ZV` and `WV` to `NQ` zeros. Returns 0.

`bend(a, b, c, d, sg, ei, l, p)` takes four i32 local dof numbers, then the reals sign sg (1.0 or minus 1.0), bending stiffness ei, length l and axial force p, and adds 4 by 4 patterns into `KE` and `GE` (12 by 12, row-major, entry (r, c) at r times 12 plus c) on the dofs (a, b, c, d) in that order. With k1 equal to ei times 12 over l cubed, k2 equal to ei times 6 times sg over l squared, k3 equal to ei times 4 over l and k4 equal to ei times 2 over l, the `KE` rows are (k1, k2, minus k1, k2), (k2, k3, minus k2, k4), (minus k1, minus k2, k1, minus k2), (k2, k4, minus k2, k3). With g1 equal to p times 6 over (5 l), g2 equal to p times sg over 10, g3 equal to p times 2 times l over 15 and g4 equal to p times l over 30, the `GE` rows are (g1, g2, minus g1, g2), (g2, g3, minus g2, minus g4), (minus g1, minus g2, g1, minus g2), (g2, minus g4, minus g2, g3). Returns 0.

`condense(r)` takes an i32 local dof and condenses it out of `KE`, `GE` and `FE`. kr is KE(r, r); when kr is at most 0.0 it returns 0 doing nothing. CV[a] is minus KE(r, a) over kr for each a below 12. For every a and b below 12 that both differ from r: GE(a, b) gains CV[a] times GE(r, b) plus GE(a, r) times CV[b] plus CV[a] times CV[b] times GE(r, r), and KE(a, b) loses KE(a, r) times KE(r, b) over kr. FE[a] gains CV[a] times FE[r] for each a differing from r. Then row r and column r of `KE` and `GE` become zeros and FE[r] becomes 0.0. Returns 0.

`local(e)` takes an i32 element and fills `KE` and `GE` (cleared to 144 zeros first) and `FE` in the local axes of its member. m is EN[3 e plus 2], s is MB[4 m plus 2], A is SP[8 s], Iy is SP[8 s plus 1], Iz is SP[8 s plus 2], J is SP[8 s plus 3], l is MS[8 m] over `NS`, E is IN[3], G is E over (2 times (1 plus IN[4])) and p is PE[e]. KE(0, 0) and KE(6, 6) get E A over l and KE(0, 6) and KE(6, 0) get minus that. KE(3, 3) and KE(9, 9) get G J over l and KE(3, 9) and KE(9, 3) get minus that. When A is above 0.0, GE(3, 3) and GE(9, 9) get p times (Iy plus Iz) over (A l) and GE(3, 9) and GE(9, 3) get minus that. Then bend(1, 5, 7, 11, 1.0, E Iz, l, p) and bend(2, 4, 8, 10, minus 1.0, E Iy, l, p). The load per length in global axes is wg_c equal to MW[4 m plus 1 plus c] plus IN[5] times 1e-9 times A times IN[6 plus c] for c 0, 1, 2; its local parts are w_a equal to the sum over c of LAM[9 m plus 3 a plus c] times wg_c for a 0, 1, 2 (wx, wy, wz). `FE` becomes (wx l over 2, wy l over 2, wz l over 2, 0.0, minus wz l squared over 12, wy l squared over 12, wx l over 2, wy l over 2, wz l over 2, 0.0, wz l squared over 12, minus wy l squared over 12). With rb equal to RL[e]: when rb bitwise-and 1 is not 0, condense(4) then condense(5); when rb bitwise-and 2 is not 0, condense(10) then condense(11); when rb bitwise-and 4 is not 0, condense(9). Returns 0.

`rot(m)` turns `KE` and `GE` from local to global axes in place with the rotation rows of member m, where lam(a, c) is LAM[9 m plus 3 a plus c]. For each of the two matrices, for each block (bi, bj) with bi and bj below 4, entry (3 bi plus p, 3 bj plus q) of the result is the sum over a and b below 3 of lam(a, p) times the old entry (3 bi plus a, 3 bj plus b) times lam(b, q). The result is computed into `TE` and then copied back. Returns 0.

`eqn(e, k)` takes an i32 element and an i32 local dof below 12 and returns 6 times POS[nd] plus k mod 6, where nd is EN[3 e] when k is below 6 and EN[3 e plus 1] otherwise.

`assemble()` sets every entry of `KV`, `GV` and `FV` to 0.0. For each element e with m equal to EN[3 e plus 2]: local(e); then for each h below 2, t below 2 and c below 3, FG[6 h plus 3 t plus c] is the sum over a below 3 of LAM[9 m plus 3 a plus c] times FE[6 h plus 3 t plus a]; FV[eqn(e, k)] gains FG[k] for each k below 12; then rot(m). For each i and j below 12, with r equal to eqn(e, i) and c equal to eqn(e, j), when r is at most c: KV[CP[c] plus r minus TOP[c]] gains KE(i, j) and GV[CP[c] plus r minus TOP[c]] loses GE(i, j). Finally FV[6 POS[j] plus k] gains FJ[6 j plus k] for each joint j and each k below 6. Returns 0.

`hold(q, mx)` takes an i32 equation and a real diagonal value and holds that equation at zero: for each r from TOP[q] to q minus 1, the entries of `KV` and `GV` at CP[q] plus r minus TOP[q] become 0.0; for each c from q plus 1 to `NQ` minus 1 with TOP[c] at most q, the entries of `KV` and `GV` at CP[c] plus q minus TOP[c] become 0.0; the `KV` diagonal at q becomes mx, the `GV` diagonal at q becomes 0.0 and FV[q] becomes 0.0. Returns 0.

`fix()` applies the supports and steadies loose rotations. mx is the largest diagonal entry KV[CP[q] plus q minus TOP[q]] over all q, and at least 1e-30. For each joint j and each k below 6 with SU[6 j plus k] equal to 1, it calls hold(6 POS[j] plus k, mx). cnt is 0. Pin joints: `RC` and `EC` are sized with the helpers to `NJ` zeros; for each member m with rb equal to MB[4 m plus 3], EC[MB[4 m]] and EC[MB[4 m plus 1]] each grow by 1, RC[MB[4 m]] grows by 1 when rb bitwise-and 1 is 0, and RC[MB[4 m plus 1]] grows by 1 when rb bitwise-and 2 is 0. For each joint j with EC[j] above 0 and RC[j] equal to 0 (every member end there is pinned), and each k from 3 to 5 with SU[6 j plus k] equal to 0, it calls hold(6 POS[j] plus k, mx) and cnt grows by 1. Then for each node n below `NT` and each k from 3 to 5, with q equal to 6 POS[n] plus k, when n is at least `NJ` or SU[6 n plus k] is 0, and the `KV` diagonal at q is at most mx times 1e-9, that diagonal becomes mx and cnt grows by 1. Returns cnt.

`factor()` factors `KV` in place into a skyline LDLt form and returns 0, or the failing equation plus 1 when a pivot is not positive. For each column j below `NQ`, with tj equal to TOP[j] and pj equal to CP[j]: for each i from tj plus 1 to j minus 1, with ti equal to TOP[i], pi equal to CP[i] and k0 the larger of ti and tj, the entry KV[pj plus i minus tj] loses the dot product of the read-only slice of `KV` from pi plus k0 minus ti and the read-only slice of `KV` from pj plus k0 minus tj, both of length i minus k0. Then ds is 0.0, and for each i from tj to j minus 1: g is KV[pj plus i minus tj], lv is g over KV[CP[i] plus i minus TOP[i]], ds gains g times lv and KV[pj plus i minus tj] becomes lv. av is KV[pj plus j minus tj] and dv is av minus ds; when av is at most 0.0 or dv is at most 1e-10 times av it returns j plus 1; otherwise KV[pj plus j minus tj] becomes dv. After every column it returns 0.

`sky_into(src, dst)` (src a read-only real slice, dst a writable real slice, both `NQ` long) solves with the factor in `KV`: dst becomes a copy of src. Forward: for each j from 0 to `NQ` minus 1, with tj equal to `TOP`[j], pj equal to `CP`[j] and m equal to j minus tj, take va as the read-only slice of `KV` from pj of length m and da as the read-only slice of dst from tj of length m; s starts at 0.0 and for each k below the length of va gets va[k] times da[k] added; then dst[j] has s subtracted. Diagonal: for each j, dst[j] becomes dst[j] divided by `KV`[`CP`[j] plus j minus `TOP`[j]]. Backward: for each j from `NQ` minus 1 down to 0, with tj, pj, m and xj equal to dst[j], take va as the read-only slice of `KV` from pj of length m and dw as the writable slice of dst from tj of length m; for each k below the length of va, dw[k] has va[k] times xj subtracted. Returns 0.

`mv(g, src, dst)` takes an i32 g (1 uses `GV`, 0 uses `KS`), a read-only real slice src and a writable real slice dst, both `NQ` long, and multiplies the symmetric skyline matrix by src: dst becomes zeros; for each column j and each i from TOP[j] to j, with v the matrix entry at CP[j] plus i minus TOP[j], dst[i] gains v times src[j], and when i differs from j, dst[j] gains v times src[i]. Returns 0.

`stress(k, s)` takes an i32 station slot and an i32 section and writes ST[8 k plus 6] and ST[8 k plus 7] from N, T, My and Mz in ST[8 k], ST[8 k plus 3], ST[8 k plus 4] and ST[8 k plus 5], with A, Iy, Iz, cy, cz, Zt and rnd from SP[8 s], SP[8 s plus 1], SP[8 s plus 2], SP[8 s plus 4], SP[8 s plus 5], SP[8 s plus 6] and SP[8 s plus 7]. sg is the absolute value of N over A (0.0 when A is 0.0) plus, when rnd is above 0.5, the square root of My squared plus Mz squared times cz over Iy, and otherwise the absolute value of My times cz over Iy plus the absolute value of Mz times cy over Iz (a term is 0.0 when its inertia is 0.0). tau is the absolute value of T over Zt when Zt is above 0.0 and 0.0 otherwise. ST[8 k plus 6] is sg and ST[8 k plus 7] is the square root of sg squared plus 3 tau squared. Returns 0.

`forces()` turns the displacements in `UV` into member forces. RJ, ST and SD become zeros. For each element e: m is EN[3 e plus 2], na is EN[3 e], nb is EN[3 e plus 1], s is MB[4 m plus 2]; local(e). For each h below 2, t below 2 and a below 3, DE[6 h plus 3 t plus a] is the sum over c below 3 of LAM[9 m plus 3 a plus c] times UV[6 POS[nd] plus 3 t plus c], where nd is na when h is 0 and nb otherwise. QE[i] is the sum over j below 12 of KE(i, j) times DE[j], minus FE[i]. PE[e] becomes (QE[6] minus QE[0]) over 2. For each h, t and c, FG[6 h plus 3 t plus c] is the sum over a below 3 of LAM[9 m plus 3 a plus c] times QE[6 h plus 3 t plus a]; when na is below `NJ`, RJ[6 na plus k] gains FG[k] for k below 6, and when nb is below `NJ`, RJ[6 nb plus k] gains FG[6 plus k]. With sl equal to e minus m times `NS` and k0 equal to m times (`NS` plus 1) plus sl: ST[8 k0 plus k] becomes minus QE[k] for k below 6, SD[3 k0 plus c] becomes UV[6 POS[na] plus c] for c below 3, and stress(k0, s). When sl is `NS` minus 1, with k1 equal to k0 plus 1: ST[8 k1 plus k] becomes QE[6 plus k] for k below 6, SD[3 k1 plus c] becomes UV[6 POS[nb] plus c], and stress(k1, s). After every element, for each joint j and k below 6: RJ[6 j plus k] becomes RJ[6 j plus k] minus FJ[6 j plus k] when SU[6 j plus k] is 1 and 0.0 otherwise, and UJ[6 j plus k] becomes UV[6 POS[j] plus k]. Returns 0.

`summary()` fills `MS` and `OUT`. For each member m, over its stations k from m times (`NS` plus 1) to m times (`NS` plus 1) plus `NS`: MS[8 m plus 1] is the smallest ST[8 k], MS[8 m plus 2] the largest ST[8 k], MS[8 m plus 3] the largest square root of ST[8 k plus 1] squared plus ST[8 k plus 2] squared, MS[8 m plus 4] the largest absolute value of ST[8 k plus 3], MS[8 m plus 5] the largest square root of ST[8 k plus 4] squared plus ST[8 k plus 5] squared, MS[8 m plus 6] the largest ST[8 k plus 6] and MS[8 m plus 7] the largest ST[8 k plus 7]. OUT[1] is the largest length of (SD[3 k], SD[3 k plus 1], SD[3 k plus 2]) over all stations and OUT[2] the member (as real) of the first station reaching it. OUT[3] is the largest MS[8 m plus 7] and OUT[4] the first member reaching it. OUT[9] is the sum over members of IN[5] times 1e-9 times SP[8 MB[4 m plus 2]] times MS[8 m]. OUT[10], OUT[11] and OUT[12] are the sums over joints of RJ[6 j], RJ[6 j plus 1] and RJ[6 j plus 2]. OUT[14] is IN[10] over OUT[3] when both are above 0.0 and 0.0 otherwise. Returns 0.

`buckle()` finds the smallest positive load factor that buckles the frame under its static axial forces in `PE`, and returns the iteration count. assemble(), fix(), `KS` becomes a copy of `KV`, and when factor() is above 0, OUT[5] becomes 0.0 and it returns 0. mi is IN[12] as int when IN[12] is above 0.0 and 2000 otherwise. sh is 0.0, mu is 0.0, tot is 0 and pass is 0. Repeat: XV[q] is sin(0.7 q plus 1.3) plus 0.5 cos(0.37 q) for each q, and 0.0 at every held equation 6 POS[j] plus k with SU[6 j plus k] equal to 1; pv is 0.0; for it from 0 to mi minus 1: mv(1, XV, ZV); mv(0, XV, WV); num is the dot product of XV and ZV and den that of XV and WV; mu is num over den when den is above 0.0 and 0.0 otherwise; sky_into(ZV, YV); YV[q] gains sh times XV[q] for each q; mx is the largest absolute value in `YV`; when mx is 0.0 the loop stops; XV[q] becomes YV[q] over mx; tot grows by 1; when it is at least 7 and the absolute value of mu minus pv is at most 1e-10 times the absolute value of mu, the loop stops; pv becomes mu. pass grows by 1; when mu is below 0.0 and pass is 1, sh becomes minus mu and it repeats; otherwise it stops repeating. OUT[5] is 1.0 over mu when mu is above 1e-15 and 0.0 otherwise. For each member m and each station s0 from 0 to `NS`, with n equal to node_of(m, s0), SB[3 (m times (`NS` plus 1) plus s0) plus c] becomes XV[6 POS[n] plus c] for c below 3; then every entry of `SB` is divided by the largest length of an (SB[3 k], SB[3 k plus 1], SB[3 k plus 2]) triple when that is above 0.0. Returns tot.

The exported function `fr_solve()`, declared with the export keyword, runs the analysis and returns 0 when it worked and 2 when the frame can move freely. build(), order(), profile(), assemble(); OUT[13] becomes fix() as real; `KS` becomes a copy of `KV`; f is factor(); when f is above 0: OUT[0] becomes 2.0, OUT[15] becomes ORD[`NT` minus 1 minus (f minus 1) divided by 6 as integer division] as real, OUT[16] becomes (f minus 1) mod 6 as real, and it returns 2. sky_into(FV, UV); forces(); summary(); OUT[7] becomes `NQ` as real and OUT[8] becomes `NV` as real. OUT[5] and OUT[6] become 0.0, and when IN[11] is above 0.5, OUT[6] becomes buckle() as real. OUT[0] becomes 0.0 and it returns 0.

The exported function `fr_bolt()`, declared with the export keyword, sizes one bolted joint after VDI 2230 from IN[20] to IN[44] and writes OUT[20] to OUT[50]; pi is the math constant and ln the natural logarithm. Inputs: d is IN[20] (nominal diameter), pt is IN[21] (pitch), rp is IN[22] (yield strength), rm is IN[23] (tensile strength), mg is IN[24] (thread friction), mk is IN[25] (head friction), dw is IN[26] when above 0.0 and 1.45 d otherwise (head bearing diameter), dh is IN[27] when above 0.0 and 1.1 d otherwise (hole diameter), lk is IN[28] (clamp length), es is IN[29] when above 0.0 and 205000.0 otherwise, ep is IN[30] when above 0.0 and es otherwise, ma is IN[31] (tightening torque, 0.0 to tighten to the limit), nu is IN[32] when above 0.0 and 0.9 otherwise, aa is IN[33] when above 0.0 and 1.5 otherwise, fa is IN[34] (axial working load), fq is IN[35] (shear load), mt is IN[36] when above 0.0 and 0.15 otherwise (interface friction), qf is IN[37] when above 0.0 and 1.0 otherwise (shear interfaces), fz is IN[38] when above 0.0 and 9.0 plus 2.0 qf otherwise (embedding in micrometers), n is IN[39] when above 0.0 and 0.5 otherwise, da is IN[40] when above 0.0 and 3.0 dw otherwise, pg is IN[41], le is IN[42], rmi is IN[43], ls is IN[44] clamped between 0.0 and lk. d2 is d minus 0.649519 pt, d3 is d minus 1.226869 pt, d0 is (d2 plus d3) over 2, as is pi d0 squared over 4, an is pi d squared over 4 and a3 is pi d3 squared over 4. kk is 1.5 times d2 over d0 times (pt over (pi d2) plus 1.155 mg); fm0 is as times nu times rp over the square root of (1 plus 3 kk squared). kt is 0.16 pt plus 0.58 d2 mg plus mk times (dw plus dh) over 4; ma0 is fm0 times kt. fmx is ma over kt when ma is above 0.0 and fm0 otherwise; fmn is fmx over aa. ds is (0.5 d plus ls plus 0.4 d) over (es an) plus (lk minus ls plus 0.5 d) over (es a3). tp is 0.362 plus 0.032 ln(the larger of lk over dw and 0.002, over 2) plus 0.153 ln(da over dw); dg is dw plus lk tp. When da is at least dg, dp is 2 ln(((dw plus dh) times (dw plus lk tp minus dh)) over ((dw minus dh) times (dw plus lk tp plus dh))) over (ep pi dh tp); otherwise dp is (2 over (dh tp) times ln(((dw plus dh) times (da minus dh)) over ((dw minus dh) times (da plus dh))) plus 4 over (da squared minus dh squared) times (lk minus (da minus dw) over tp)) over (ep pi). ph is n dp over (ds plus dp); fsa is ph fa; fpa is fa minus fsa; fzl is fz times 0.001 over (ds plus dp); fkr is fmn minus fzl minus fpa; fsm is fmx plus fsa. sz is fsm over as; mgt is fmx times d2 over 2 times (pt over (pi d2) plus 1.155 mg); tau is mgt over (pi d0 cubed over 16); sred is the square root of sz squared plus 3 times (0.5 tau) squared; sa is fsa over (2 as); sasv is 0.85 times (150.0 over d plus 45.0); ap is pi times (dw squared minus dh squared) over 4; pr is fsm over ap; fke is fq over (qf mt) when fq is above 0.0 and 0.0 otherwise; lrq is rm times as over (0.6 rmi times 0.75 pi d) when rmi is above 0.0 and 0.0 otherwise. OUT[20] to OUT[50] become, in order: d2, d3, as, fm0, ma0, fmx, fmn, ds, dp, ph, fsa, fpa, fzl, fkr, fsm, sred, rp over sred, sa, sasv, sasv over sa (0.0 when sa is 0.0), pr, pg over pr (0.0 when pg is 0.0), fke, fkr times qf times mt over fq (0.0 when fq is 0.0), lrq, le over lrq (0.0 when lrq or le is 0.0), tau, tp, dg, kt, and 1.0 when fkr is at most 0.0 and 0.0 otherwise. Returns 0.

`main()` does nothing.
